WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.367
Welcome to Module 2, Designing Relational Databases.

00:00:04.367 --> 00:00:11.797
In this module, we are going to focus on the structure that makes reliable SQL and analytics possible

00:00:11.797 --> 00:00:17.912
later. Before we worry about writing long queries, we need to decide what each table represents,

00:00:17.912 --> 00:00:24.742
what one row means, how tables connect, and what rules the database should protect automatically.

00:00:24.742 --> 00:00:31.677
The examples use a small outpatient clinic, but the design ideas apply to many domains such as retail,

00:00:31.677 --> 00:00:32.862
education, finance, and operations.

00:00:32.862 --> 00:00:38.592
You will also see small PostgreSQL examples that run directly in the browser.

00:00:38.592 --> 00:00:45.772
When a code slide appears, run it here first, inspect the result, and if you want more practice,

00:00:45.772 --> 00:00:48.922
use Copy and paste the same SQL into the pgAdmin Query Tool.

00:00:48.922 --> 00:00:52.282
The goal is not to memorize a complete schema.

00:00:52.282 --> 00:00:59.037
The goal is to understand the design decisions behind the schema.

00:00:59.037 --> 00:01:03.917
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example, a clinic system separating patients,

00:01:03.917 --> 00:01:08.982
encounters, clinicians, and billing.

00:01:08.982 --> 00:01:14.222
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:01:14.222 --> 00:01:17.792
analyst can understand our work.

00:01:17.792 --> 00:01:24.877
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to

00:01:24.877 --> 00:01:28.562
defend later: what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:01:28.562 --> 00:01:32.596
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
